{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Second independent occurrence — different session, different commit\n\nSame assertion, same line, reached by a different route, which rules out anything specific to\nthe original worktree or commit.\n\n- Session `6a3fecbb-7c5e-4e07-b1ba-7eb2bd09cb7c`, worktree `impl-cb1b`, commit `f2ced750d1b`.\n- `make verify` ran 2 h 18 min (8293.97 s) against a documented 25–30 min, with a second\n  `make verify` running concurrently on the same host, each at `-n 4 --dist worksteal`.\n- `tests/unit/test_fetch_common_dir_coordination.py:431: AssertionError`, 0 races in 40\n  rounds.\n- That commit adds MCP async gate tools and touches neither this test nor the code under\n  test.\n\nTwo sightings, two sessions, two unrelated commits, both on a saturated host and both with a\nwall time 5–9× nominal. The common factor is host load, not any change under test.\n\n## Which half of the test fails — worth stating explicitly\n\nThe failure is in the **positive control**, not in the subject. The test is in two parts:\n\n- `:426-431` — up to 40 **uncoordinated** rounds, assert a race was seen at least once.\n- `:433-436` — 8 **coordinated** rounds, assert zero races.\n\nExecution stops at `:431`, so the assertion that actually checks `fetch_coordination_lock`\n(`:436`) is never evaluated. Nothing in either sighting is evidence that the coordination lock\nis broken — a useful thing to have recorded, so a future reader does not re-investigate the\nlock.\n\nThe mechanism is visible in the test's own comment at `:424-425`: \"High base rate → a small\ncap is more than enough; hitting the cap with zero races would itself be astronomically\nunlikely.\" The 6 workers release from a `threading.Barrier(n)` and then each runs a real\n`git fetch`. That base rate is high only while the threads genuinely overlap; on a saturated\nhost they are descheduled after the barrier, the fetches serialize, and the CAS never\ncollides. The base rate is a function of host load, and the test treats it as a constant.\n\n## One acceptance criterion worth adding\n\nThe current ACs are satisfiable by weakening the control until it cannot fail — for example\nby deleting the uncoordinated half outright. That would make the red go away while removing\nthe only thing proving the lock is load-bearing. Suggest adding:\n\n- [ ] The test still FAILS when `fetch_coordination_lock` is disabled or stubbed, demonstrated\n      with a red run. A test that can no longer fail has not been fixed.\n\n## Related but distinct\n\nThe same `make verify` also failed two CLI facade tests on a stale stderr logging handler —\n`cded-334b-a742-4082` (bald-diagnostic-cricket), a confirmed and separate root cause. These\ntwo bugs share only the loaded host, not a mechanism.\n\n`1a3b-e678-0eeb-4230` was filed from this session before this ticket surfaced; it is now\nlinked `duplicates` here and archived.\n"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1793826493806637344,"uuid":"d52b7e93-3a6c-47de-a959-eb21cde1e8c2"}